n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n address-family ipv4 unicast\\n neighbor 10.0.7.7 activate\\n neighbor 1.1.1.1 activate\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, the BGP family configuration is split into:\\n1. Entering BGP configuration mode (router bgp)\\n2. Specifying the address family (ipv4 unicast in this case)\\n3. Activating the neighbors for that address family\\n\\nThe AS number (100) is taken from the group name \"AS100\" in the original Juniper configuration, and the neighbor addresses are derived from the neighbor statements in the original config.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.0.7.7 activate'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 1.1.1.1 activate', 'parent_command': ['vpdn-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'vpdn-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Cisco command for the given Juniper BGP family configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n address-family ipv4 unicast\\n neighbor 10.0.7.7 activate\\n neighbor 1.1.1.1 activate\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, the BGP family configuration is split into:\\n1. Entering BGP configuration mode (router bgp)\\n2. Specifying the address family (ipv4 unicast in this case)\\n3. Activating the neighbors for that address family\\n\\nThe AS number (100) is taken from the group name \"AS100\" in the original Juniper configuration, and the neighbor addresses are derived from the neighbor statements in the original config.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 1.1.1.1 activate'}]]": 1,
